 coming  through and i can actually drill down  into one of these  and see exactly where all the time  is being spent like what's being called  and how long it's taking so if i need to  get information and about the actual  operation of of what's going on i can  very easily  figure out where i need to make  optimizations or i need to make changes  to fix things the other thing i want to  show you is  is grafana and if you look  you can get a very detailed view of the  information about the system  of the memory cpu you know all the  information about that  i can also show you  a service dashboard on end service  and it shows you request volume success  rate things like that  so we have we have very detailed  information about what's going on in the  system  and all this is just absolutely built  into istio  and available at all times and can be  extended as well uh various plugins  so i'm going to hand this back to  non-demand  so what we have seen now is how to use  istio to do basic service management  right what you've seen with service  management needs  are the basic ability to do mpls so you  can encrypt and authenticate your  traffic  i think you want to have this  operational agility so you can do a b  testing you can do canadian releases  all of that and third you want to do  policy enforcement  they apply to all services  right and this is back to that so how  does  how does service management really tie  in with api management  so first let us establish what is api  management  and then let's talk about how service  management compares  with api management api management is  the ability  to provide an api catalog for discovery  if you go back to this architecture here  this is the  application that scott wrote it's got a  currency service that's got a checkout  service  very frequently in enterprises the team  that builds the currency service is  different from the team that build the  checkout service now that team  that builds the currency service will 